# Rendered per-instance by bin/season-units.sh into
#   ~/.config/systemd/user/nwn-season-server@<instance>.service.d/priority.conf
#
# WHY THIS IS PER-INSTANCE AND NOT A SHARED TEMPLATE DROP-IN
# ---------------------------------------------------------
# It used to be `nwn-season-server@.service.d/priority.conf` with CPUWeight=40 /
# IOWeight=40, to stop the server's .NET/Anvil module load starving gnome-shell
# during the *boot* contention window. Two things were wrong with that:
#
#   1. A drop-in has no time limit. The "boot window" weight applied FOREVER.
#   2. It sat on the @ template, so it hit every instance -- including the LIVE
#      season -- and the installed symlink pointed at whichever repo ran
#      --install last (in practice the dev repo governed the prod server).
#
# Net effect: the default weight is 100, so the live production game server was
# running at 40 while builds, the wiki refresh, Claude and gnome-shell all ran
# at 100+. The kernel was faithfully starving prod in favour of the toolchain --
# which is exactly the in-game lag players kept reporting.
#
# Boot-time desktop responsiveness is now handled where it belongs: the heavy
# boot job (nwn-season-wiki-publish@) already yields via its own drop-in
# (CPUWeight=5, Nice=19, 150s ExecStartPre delay), and build tooling runs under
# bin/lowprio in background.slice. The game server no longer has to pay for it.
#
# Weights are PROPORTIONAL and only bite under contention, so a high weight
# costs nothing on an idle box.
@ROLE_COMMENT@
[Service]
CPUWeight=@CPU_WEIGHT@
IOWeight=@IO_WEIGHT@
@EXTRA@
